All three scored 14 of 14 on the spec probe, including the clauses that carry the design:
Observed(0) stays a measurement rather than becoming Missing; an overflowing count is Missing
rather than a wrapped value; and Launcher::Unknown on a log that HAPPENS to contain the marker is
Missing, because we do not know the string means the same thing for a launcher we do not
recognise.

VERIFIED ON REAL LOGS, which is the test that counts: run over every codex-luna log on disk, the
merged parser reads a token count from 46 of 46, none missing --

    fnd-core--codex-luna.log        37968
    dmn-proto--codex-luna.log       58224
    wt-provision--codex-luna.log    48791
    fnd-store--codex-luna.log       68891

That is the entire UNPRICED 32/32 gap on the Pareto board, and more besides. The board could not
place its best-performing arm on the frontier because nothing had ever read a number the launcher
prints on every run.

The only critique, codex-luna on glm-53-flash: NO MATERIAL DEFECTS, with a judgement worth
keeping -- the parser strips every comma before parsing, so malformed grouping like `13,08`
reads as 1308. It then declines to call that a defect, correctly: "the task does not require
validating grouping ... treating this as a defect would impose a stricter contract than the
specification states." That is a critic reasoning about the spec's authority rather than its own
preference, and it is the right answer.

CROSSX VOIDED, on codex-luna's diagonal alone. I checked whether that was its code: grafting its
own suite into its own file by hand compiles cleanly. So the fault is in crossx's strip-then-graft
path, another instance of the P1 already open, and not a mark against the arm.

glm-53-flash on the usual thin margin: the most tests (38 to 33 to 31), and the only free route
of the three.

